          %h2#introduction Introduction
          %p
            A
            %strong Polish space
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            that’s
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphic
            to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separable+space"} separable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+space"} complete
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second+countable+space"} second countable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+compact+space"} locally compact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+space"} Hausdorff space
            is a Polish space, among others.
          %p
            Polish spaces, or the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+spaces"} measurable spaces
            that they define (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+Borel+spaces"}> standard Borel spaces
            ) provide a useful framework for doing
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+theory"} measure theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+space"} measurable space
            with its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-algebra"} sigma-algebra
            of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+set"} Borel set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+set"} countable
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinality"} cardinality
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum"} continuum
          %p
            Why are Polish spaces ‘not very big’? In other words, why are there none with cardinality exceeding the continuum? As with any separable metric space, it’s because any Polish space has a countable dense subset and you can write any point as a limit of a sequence of points in this subset. So, you only need a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequence"} sequence
            of integers to specify any point in a Polish space. More sharply, see Lemma
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#baire"}
            below.
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              The primordial example (and in practice, one of the most convenient) is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set-theoretic+Baire+space"} Baire space
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subspace+topology"} subspace topology
              inherited from the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real line
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}^\mathbb{N}
              via regular
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continued+fraction"} continued fraction
              expansions, and the latter is metrizable by a complete metric where the distance between two sequences
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a = (a_1, a_2, \ldots)
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b = (b_1, b_2, \ldots)
              of positive integers is given by the formula
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(a, b) = 1/2^n
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              is the least integer such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mi n
                      %mo ≠
                      %msub
                        %mi b
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_n \neq b_n
          %p
            The convenience of Baire space is attested to by the fact that in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descriptive+set+theory"} descriptive set theory
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Borel"}
            below.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            Much of the material here is adapted from
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Marker"} Marker
            %a{:href => "#Kechris"} Kechris
            as a main source.
          %ul
            %li
              A countable product
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mi n
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = \prod_n X_n
              of Polish spaces is Polish. Indeed, each
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_n
              is metrizable by a complete metric
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_n
              that takes values in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0, 1]
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              by
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mi n
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 1
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mn 2
                            %mrow
                              %mi n
                              %mo +
                              %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(f, g) = \sum_n \frac1{2^{n+1}} d_n(f(n), g(n)).
              %p
                The metric topology on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                coincides with the product topology.
          %p
            In particular, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+cube"} Hilbert cube
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0, 1]^\mathbb{N}
            is Polish.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A subspace of a Polish space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is Polish iff it is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-delta+set"} G-delta set
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "#Marker"} Marker
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X, Y
                are Polish and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is locally compact, then the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponentiable+space"} exponential
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Y
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^X
                (the space of continuous maps with the
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact-open+topology"} compact-open topology
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#KerrLi2016"} Kerr and Li
          %h3#universal_polish_spaces “Universal” Polish spaces
          #hilbert.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p Any Polish space is homeomorphic to a subspace of the Hilbert cube.
          %p
            In fact, this is true of any
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separable+space"} separable metrizable space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \N
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mi δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_\delta
              subsets
            of the Hilbert cube.
          #baire.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited"} inhabited
              Polish space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              admits a continuous surjection from Baire space.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_sketch Proof (sketch)
            %p
              Construct by induction a collection of closed sets (balls)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_s
              indexed over
              %em finite
              sequences
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
              of positive integers, with the following properties:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  For the empty sequence
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi e
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi C
                            %mi e
                          %mo =
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_e = X
              %li
                %p
                  For nonempty sequences
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi s
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi diam
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ≤
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} diam(C_s) \leq 1/n
                  where
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo =
                          %mi length
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi s
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = length(s)
              %li
                %p
                  Letting
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi s
                        %mo ,
                        %mi k
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (s, k)
                  denote the extension of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
                  obtained by appending to
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
                  the final element
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi k
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mi s
                        %mo =
                        %munderover
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                          %mrow
                            %mi k
                            %mo =
                            %mn 0
                          %mn ∞
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi s
                            %mo ,
                            %mi k
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ;
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_s = \bigcup_{k=0}^\infty C_{(s, k)};
              %li
                %p
                  If
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi t
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
                  extends
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi s
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi center
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi C
                            %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ∈
                          %msub
                            %mi C
                            %mi s
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} center(C_t) \in C_s
            %p
              Then define
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: B \to X
              where for each infinite sequence
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi ℕ
                        %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in \mathbb{N}^\mathbb{N}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋂
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi s
                    %mo :
                    %mi a
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi extends
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(a) = \bigcap \{C_s: a\; extends\; s\}.
            %p
              One may check that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is continuous and surjective.
          %h3#cantorbendixson_rank Cantor-Bendixson rank
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \subseteq X
            be a closed subset of a Polish space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+ordinal"} countable ordinals
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal"} ordinal
            analysis.
          %p
            For a subset
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subseteq X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in A
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit+point"} limit point
            if
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Cl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∖
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in Cl(A \setminus \{x\})
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in A
            that is not a limit point of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is called an
            %em isolated point
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              The
              %strong Cantor-Bendixson derivative
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is the set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C' \subseteq C
              of limit points relative to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
              the iterated derivative
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\alpha
              is defined by recursion:
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mn 0
                      %mo =
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^0 = C
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mrow
                          %mi α
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mi α
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\alpha + 1} = (C^\alpha)'
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi α
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋂
                      %mrow
                        %mi β
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi α
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi β
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\alpha = \bigcap_{\beta \lt \alpha} C^\beta
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
              is a limit ordinal.
          %p
            Since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi β
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta \lt \alpha
            implies
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi β
                    %mo ⊇
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\beta \supseteq C^\alpha
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            for which
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi α
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mrow
                        %mi α
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\alpha = C^{\alpha + 1}
            %em Cantor-Bendixson rank
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perfect+set"} perfect set
            is a closed set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            such that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo =
                    %mi C
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C = C'
          #cantor.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              For each
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+set"} nonempty
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perfect+set"} perfect set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              in a Polish space
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 2
                      %mi ℕ
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: \mathbf{2}^\mathbb{N} \to P
              from
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cantor+space"} Cantor space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum"} continuum
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              be the complete infinite binary tree, whose infinite paths from the root correspond to points in Cantor space
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mn 2
                        %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{2}^\mathbb{N}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              (a finite sequence of
              = succeed "\u2018s" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              = succeed "\u2019s)" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_s
              with the following properties:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mi e
                        %mo =
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_e = X
                  for the empty sequence
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi e
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi U
                              %mi t
                          %mo ¯
                        %mo ⊆
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mi s
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widebar{U_t} \subseteq U_s
                  if
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
                  is an initial segment of
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi t
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
              %li
                %p
                  For the two children
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi s
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (s, 0)
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi s
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (s, 1)
                  of
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi s
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi s
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 0
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_{(s, 0)}
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi s
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_{(s, 1)}
                  are disjoint,
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi diam
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ≤
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} diam(U_s) \leq 1/n
                  where
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                  is the length of (nonempty)
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi s
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mi s
                        %mo ∩
                        %mi P
                        %mo ≠
                        %mi ∅
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_s \cap P \neq \emptyset
                  for each
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi s
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            %p
              Indeed, if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_s
              has been constructed, and given
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mi s
                      %mo ∩
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in U_s \cap P
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi s
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_0, x_1 \in U_s \cap P
              since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              is perfect. We can easily find disjoint neighborhoods
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi s
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi s
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_{(s, 0)}, U_{(s, 1)}
              of these points respectively with the required properties.
            %p
              Then for each path
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mn 2
                        %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p = (a_0, a_1, \ldots) \in \mathbf{2}^\mathbb{N}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋂
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo ⪯
                        %mi p
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i(p) = \bigcap_{s \preceq p} U_s
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo ⪯
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s \preceq p
              means
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
              is an initial segment of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+sequence"} Cauchy sequence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              is clearly injective by the disjointness of open sets of children, and it is easy to see
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              is continuous.
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              a closed subset of a Polish space
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ∖
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \setminus C^\alpha
              is at most countable.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i
              be a countable basis of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi β
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi α
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta \lt \alpha
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi β
                    %mo ∖
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mrow
                        %mi β
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in C^\beta \setminus C^{\beta + 1}
              is an isolated point, so we can find an
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_{i(x)}
              in the basis such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∩
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mi β
                      %mo ∖
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mrow
                          %mi β
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_{i(x)} \cap (C^\beta \setminus C^{\beta + 1}) = \{x\}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \mapsto i(x)
              is injective, so each
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi β
                    %mo ∖
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mrow
                        %mi β
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\beta \setminus C^{\beta + 1}
              is countable. Similarly, whenever
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi β
                    %mo ∖
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mrow
                        %mi β
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\beta \setminus C^{\beta + 1}
              is nonempty, we can find a basis element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mrow
                        %mi j
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi β
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_{j(\beta)}
              that isolates one of its points (say
              = succeed ")," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_j
              cannot isolate any point of an earlier
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi γ
                    %mo ∖
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mrow
                        %mi γ
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\gamma \setminus C^{\gamma + 1}
              since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              is a limit point of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mi γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\gamma
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi β
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi β
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta \mapsto j(\beta)
              is an injective map, so that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
              must be a countable ordinal, and the collection
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi C
                    %mo ∖
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi α
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                      %mrow
                        %mi β
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi α
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi β
                    %mo ∖
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mrow
                        %mi β
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \coloneqq C \setminus C^\alpha = \bigcup_{\beta \lt \alpha} C^\beta \setminus C^{\beta + 1}
              is (at most) countable.
          #CH.num_cor
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p
              A closed set in a Polish space is the disjoint union
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo ∪
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \cup F
              of a perfect set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              and a finite or countable set
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℵ
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \aleph_0
              or the continuum
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ℵ
                          %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c = 2^{\aleph_0}
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum+hypothesis"} continuum hypothesis
              for closed sets).
          %h3#borel_isomorphism_between_polish_spaces Borel isomorphism between Polish spaces
          %p
            Recall that a function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: X \to Y
            between topological spaces is
            %em Borel
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}(V)
            is a Borel set in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            for every open
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
          .num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_2 Lemma
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A, B
              are countably infinite
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_1
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separation+axiom"} spaces
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: A \to B
              is a Borel isomorphism.
          %p
            For, the inverse image
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}(U)
            of an open set, being countable, is an
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_\sigma
            set. In particular, any two denumerable Polish spaces are Borel isomorphic.
          .num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_3 Lemma
            %p
              The unit interval
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0, 1]
              and Cantor space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^\mathbb{N}
              are Borel isomorphic.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo ⊆
                    %msup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 2
                      %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \subseteq \mathbf{2}^\mathbb{N}
              be the set of
              = succeed "-sequences" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0, 1)
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mi n
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mi n
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mn 2
                          %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(a_1, a_2, \ldots) = \sum_n \frac{a_n}{2^n}
            %p
              maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mi ℕ
                    %mo ∖
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^\mathbb{N} \setminus E
              homeomorphically onto the space of non-(dyadic rational) numbers in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0, 1]
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo :
                      %mi E
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi dyadic
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi rationals
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g: E \to \{dyadic\; rationals\}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
              defines a Borel isomorphism
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                      %mo :
                      %msup
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mn 2
                        %mi ℕ
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h: \mathbf{2}^\mathbb{N} \to [0, 1]
          #subset.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              Any Polish space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is Borel isomorphic to a Borel subset of Cantor space.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_4 Proof
            %p There is a sequence of maps
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ↪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mi ℕ
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi h
                          %mi ℕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 2
                      %mi ℕ
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi ℕ
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi j
                    %msup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 2
                      %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \hookrightarrow [0, 1]^\mathbb{N} \stackrel{h^\mathbb{N}}{\to} (\mathbf{2}^{\mathbb{N}})^\mathbb{N} \stackrel{j}{\to} \mathbf{2}^\mathbb{N}
            %p
              where the first map is an inclusion of a
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mi δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_\delta
              set, the second is induced from a Borel isomorphism
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
          #Borel.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p Any two Polish spaces of the same cardinality are Borel isomorphic.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_5 Proof
            %p
              It suffices to prove this for uncountable Polish spaces (which have continuum cardinality, as we saw in Corollary
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CH"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is Borel isomorphic to Cantor space. By Proposition
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#subset"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 2
                      %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: X \to \mathbf{2}^{\mathbb{N}}
              that maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              Borel isomorphically onto its image, and by Proposition
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#cantor"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 2
                      %mi ℕ
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j: \mathbf{2}^{\mathbb{N}} \to X
              that maps Cantor space Borel isomorphically (even homeomorphically) onto its image. The rest is just a matter of checking that at least one of the proofs of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cantor-Schroeder-Bernstein+theorem"} Cantor-Schroeder-Bernstein theorem
              applies to this Borel context.
            %p
              Indeed, as explained
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Cantor-Schroeder-Bernstein+theorem#alt"} here
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo =
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋂
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ¬
                    %msub
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi j
                    %mo ¬
                    %msub
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi i
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S = \bigcap_{n \geq 0} (\neg \exists_j \neg \exists_i)^n(X)
            %p
              Each of the direct image maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists_i
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists_j
              takes Borel sets to Borel sets, since their inverses
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi i
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi j
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i^{-1}, j^{-1}
              are Borel functions on their domains. So each of the iterates
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ¬
                    %msub
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi j
                    %mo ¬
                    %msub
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi i
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\neg \exists_j \neg \exists_i)^n(X)
              is a Borel set and so is their countable intersection
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              is a fixed point of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %msub
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi j
                    %mo ¬
                    %msub
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg \exists_j \neg \exists_i
              and so we construct a Borel isomorphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 2
                      %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h: X \to \mathbf{2}^{\mathbb{N}}
              as
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi x
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↦
                          %mtd
                            %mi i
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mi if
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∈
                            %mi S
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↦
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi j
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mi if
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∈
                            %mo ¬
                            %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    x &amp; \mapsto &amp; i(x) &amp; if\; x \in S \\
                    \&amp; \mapsto &amp; j^{-1}(x) &amp; if\; x \in \neg S
                    }
            %p
              following the proof of the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cantor-Schroeder-Bernstein+theorem"} Cantor-Schroeder-Bernstein theorem
          %p
            For another proof, see theorem 3.1.1 of
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Ber"} Berberian
          %h2#further_examples Further examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The classical
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi L
                          %mi p
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lebesgue+space"} spaces
                for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \lt \infty
                are Polish spaces.
            %li
              %p
                For a metric space
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(X)
                be the set of nonempty
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compact
                subsets of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+metric"} Hausdorff metric
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is a separable complete metric space, then so is
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi K
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(X)
            %li
              %p
                A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+compact+Hausdorff+space"} locally compact Hausdorff space
                is Polish iff it is second-countable.
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is Polish, then the space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(X)
                of Borel probability measures
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
                equipped with the Prokhorov metric is also Polish. Definitions: for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subset X
                nonempty, put
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo ,
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi inf
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi d
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo ,
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo :
                        %mi a
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(x, A) = \inf \{d(x, a): a \in A\}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                      %mo &gt;
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \gt 0
                define
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mi α
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                      %mo :
                      %mi d
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi α
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\alpha = \{x \in X: d(x, A) \lt \alpha\}
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ∅
                          %mi α
                        %mo =
                        %mi ∅
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \emptyset_\alpha = \emptyset
                %em Prokhorov metric
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_P
                by
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi μ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ν
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi inf
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi α
                      %mo &gt;
                      %mn 0
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mo ∀
                        %mrow
                          %mi Borel
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi A
                      %mi μ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi ν
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mi α
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo +
                      %mi α
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi and
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi ν
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi μ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mi α
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo +
                      %mi α
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_P(\mu, \nu) = \inf \{\alpha \gt 0: \forall_{Borel\; A} \mu(A) \lt \nu(A_\alpha) + \alpha \; and \; \nu(A) \lt \mu(A_\alpha) + \alpha\}.
              %p
                Then the map
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi u
                        %mi X
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u_X: X \to P(X)
                sending
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+measure"} Dirac measure
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi δ
                        %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta_x
                is continuous. If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_n
                is a countable dense set of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo =
                          %mn 1
                        %mi n
                      %msub
                        %mi q
                        %mi n
                      %msub
                        %mi δ
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi x
                            %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{i=1}^n q_n \delta_{x_n}
                (with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ≤
                      %msub
                        %mi q
                        %mi n
                      %mo ≤
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \leq q_n \leq 1
                and
                = succeed ")" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                          %mi i
                        %msub
                          %mi q
                          %mi i
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_i q_i = 1
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(X)
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giry+monad"} Giry monad
            %li
              %p
                Spaces of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure+in+model+theory"} structures
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/models"} models
                (in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+theory"} model theory
                sense), and spaces of
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                %span.newWikiWord
                  types
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/n-type+%28model+theory%29"} ?
                (again in the model theory sense), quite often provide examples of Polish spaces. For example, if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                is a countable language (a countable
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/signature+%28in+logic%29"} signature
                = succeed "-structures" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi L
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                on the countable universe
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℕ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mi ϕ
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi M
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Struct
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi L
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi M
                      %mo ⊧
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_\phi = \{M \in Struct(L): M \models \phi\}
              %p
                where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
                is a quantifier-free sentence, is a Polish space homeomorphic to the product space
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi relations
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi R
                      %msup
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mn 2
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi ℕ
                            %mrow
                              %mi arity
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi R
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ×
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi functions
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi f
                      %msup
                        %mi ℕ
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi ℕ
                            %mrow
                              %mi arity
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi f
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_{relations\; R} \mathbf{2}^{\mathbb{N}^{arity(R)}} \times \prod_{functions\; f} \mathbb{N}^{\mathbb{N}^{arity(f)}}
              %p
                (taking constants to be functions of arity
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
                in the signature).
          %p As an example of the last principle, we have a kind of continuum hypothesis for substructures:
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_4 Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be a countable structure of a language; then the number of substructures of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is either countable or the continuum.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_6 Proof
            %p
              A subset of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is specified by its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic+function"} characteristic function
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi χ
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mn 2
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi \in 2^X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^X
              is regarded as a Polish space. In order for the subset
              %em not
              to support a substructure, then must be some function symbol
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              of the language, say of arity
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_1, \ldots, a_n
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi χ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi(a_i) = 1
              for all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi χ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi(f_X(a_1, \ldots, a_n)) = 0
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo ;
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mi …
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mi n
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi ω
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_{f; a_1, \ldots, a_n} \coloneqq \{\omega \in 2^X: \; \omega(a_1) = 1, \ldots, \omega(a_n) = 1, \; \omega(f_X(a_1, \ldots, a_n)) = 0\}
            %p
              would thus contain
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi χ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi
              and also exclude any substructure; we conclude that the collection of substructures forms a closed subset
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mn 2
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^X
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CH"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|C|}
              is either countable or the continuum.
          %h2#polish_spaces_of_mappings Polish spaces of mappings
          %p
            In his study of wild knots,
            %a{:href => "#Kulnikov"} Kulnikov
            studied the space of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/embeddings"} embeddings
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^3
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            in a Polish space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            is again a Polish space. For this we first give the space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Y
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^X
            the metric
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mi sup
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(f,g) = \sup_{x \in X} d(f(x),g(x))
          %p
            where the supremum is well-defined because
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is compact. The topology coming from this metric is called the ‘’uniform topology’’.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_5 Proposition
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a compact Polish space and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              is a Polish space, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Y
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^X
              with the above metric is a Polish space, and its uniform topology is the compact-open topology.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_7 Proof
            %p
              Given any compact metric space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and metric space
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Y
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^X
              agrees with the compact-open topology. If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is any locally compact Polish space and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              is a Polish space,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Y
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^X
              with its compact-open topology is a Polish space, by A.10 of
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#KerrLi2016"} Kerr and Li
          %p We then have:
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_6 Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be a compact Polish space and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              a Polish space. Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Emb
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊆
                    %msup
                      %mi Y
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Emb}(X,Y) \subseteq Y^X
              be the space of embeddings of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Emb
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Emb}(X,Y)
              is a Polish space.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_8 Proof
            %p
              Since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is compact and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              is Hausdorff, a map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to Y
              is an embedding, i.e.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is a homeomorphism from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              to its image, if and only if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is injective. Thus we have
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Emb
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi Y
                      %mi X
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≠
                    %mi y
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≠
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Emb}(X,Y) = \{ f \in Y^X\; \vert \; (\forall x, y \in X) \; x \ne y \Rightarrow f(x) \ne f(y) \}
            %p
              To show
